  :adr/body "# ADR-2607256000: murakumo task plane — fleet-wide batch execution, verified live

**Status**: accepted
**Date**: 2026-07-25
**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki
**Scope**: `orgs/kotoba-lang/murakumo`

## Context

ADR-2607071400 fixed the positioning: the murakumo family occupies the k8s /
wasmCloud+wadm slot in this stack, and its equivalence table maps manifest,
reconciler, scheduler, kubelet, etcd, service mesh and CLI onto real code.
Asked whether a Ray equivalent also exists, the honest answer (2026-07-25) was
no. `kotoba-lang/com-ray` exists but is one of the ~1,027 generated vendor
compat facades (ADR-2607041500, batch26 `ray-compat`): its schema is
Device/Kernel/MemoryAlloc/InferenceJob/Engine/Utilization CRUD, with none of
Ray's task/actor/object-store model. The Ray-shaped roles were scattered —
`murakumo.infer.plan` (exo-style layer sharding), `murakumo.infer.schedule`
(whole-job render farm), `cloud-murakumo` (GPU serverless, Modal-equivalent),
`kototama` (single-node actor host) — and no component fanned a BATCH of
short-lived units of work across the fleet and gathered the results.

That is the k8s-Job / `ray.remote` shape, and it was the one row missing from
the equivalence table. The owner asked to design it, implement it, and verify
through the CLI that murakumo actually distributes execution.

## Decision

Add a **task plane** to murakumo, beside — not inside — the app reconciler.

- **`src/murakumo/task/plan.cljc` (pure)** — eligibility uses the same
  vocabulary as `reconcile/plan.cljc` (`:labels` all match, `:roles` all
  present) plus `:min-mem-bytes`, an explicit node allow-list and
  `:exclude-nodes` for retries. Placement is **least-FILLED first**: the
  assigned/slots ratio dominates, so a 32-core box takes proportionally more
  than a 10-core mini; live `load1`, then memory, then name break ties. No
  clock and no RNG, so a plan is reproducible and auditable.
- **`src/murakumo/task/exec.cljs` (nbb shell)** — per-node bounded concurrency
  (`slots` = `min(cores, max-slots)`), per-task timeout with SIGKILL, and the
  fleet probe (cores / RAM / load1 / reachability).
- **`src/murakumo/task.cljs`** — the `task` CLI: `probe`, `plan` (pure preview,
  nothing executed), `run`, `report`, plus an append-only run ledger.
- Runtime choice follows the repo rule: the planner is portable `.cljc`, the
  shell is **nbb** — no new `bb.edn`, no new `.sh` or `.mjs` (ADR-2607173000).

Failure model is Ray's task retry, stated honestly: a failed task is re-placed
on a **different** node, and a task that succeeds on retry is a **succeeded
task** — the run's verdict is the final attempt per task id, with
`attempts`/`retried` reported separately. There is no lineage re-execution.

## Verification (live, 2026-07-25)

Fleet probe: **11 of 12 nodes reachable, 132 cores, 207G RAM** (xavier
unreachable for the reason already documented in `fleet.edn`).

| check | result |
|---|---|
| 22 trivial tasks | 11 nodes, 519ms wall for 5195ms of task time = **10.01x** |
| 22 CPU-bound tasks (800MB SHA-256 each) | 2779ms wall for 16871ms task time = **6.07x**, identical digest on arm64 and x86_64 |
| label-constrained (`--labels tier=gpu`) | all 4 tasks on `gad` only, 3.81x from intra-node slots |
| injected failure on `asher` | retried on `gad`; tasks=11 ok=11 failed=0 attempts=12 retried=1 |
| permanent failure (`--attempts 1`) | ok=0 failed=3, CLI exit 1 |
| timeout (`--timeout-ms 3000`) | killed at 3004ms, classified as timeout |
| unsatisfiable placement | reported as UNSCHEDULABLE, not silently dropped |
| heterogeneous batch from file | per-task placement honoured (gpu → gad, 32G floor → gad, mesh role → judah) |
| offline unit tests | 13 tests / 62 assertions green under nbb, no SSH |

So: **yes — murakumo now distributes real execution across the fleet through
the CLI**, and the parallelism is measured rather than asserted.

## What verification found (two real defects, one regression)

1. **Tailscale SSH on the macOS nodes does not propagate the remote exit
   status.** `ssh asher 'exit 7'` returns 0 and `ssh asher false` returns 0,
   while the Linux node returns 7 and 1; `ssh -v` shows a Tailscale SSH server
   on both, so this is the macOS Tailscale SSH path, not the shell config. Any
   automation trusting ssh's exit code silently reports success on 10 of the 11
   reachable nodes. Fixed here by running every remote command in a subshell
   that echoes an in-band `__murakumo_rc=` sentinel which wins over ssh's own
   code. **The other murakumo SSH paths (provision/status/deploy/infer) were
   not audited for this and may carry the same false-success risk.**
2. **`int` in ClojureScript is a 32-bit bit-or**, which silently zeroed probed
   byte counts (17179869184 becomes 0). Round instead.
3. **The murakumo CLI has had no runnable entrypoint since the bb retirement.**
   ADR-2607173000 Wave 3 replaced `bb.edn` with `scripts/tasks.edn` but carried
   over only three shell-out tasks; every control-plane entrypoint
   (nodes/status/provision/mesh/deploy/reconcile/infer/model/token/dash/cloud/
   overlay/test) was dropped, and those namespaces cannot run under plain
   `clojure` either (they require `babashka.process` and `cheshire`, absent
   from `deps.edn`). `task` is the first entrypoint ported to nbb; the rest
   need the same port. This is recorded, not fixed here.

## Consequences

- The equivalence table of ADR-2607071400 gains a row: **k8s Job / Ray tasks →
  `murakumo task`** (that ADR stays the positioning reference and is not
  rewritten; this ADR is the implementation record).
- Batch work on this fleet (benchmarks, fan-out probes, render/ingest batches)
  has a first-class, auditable path instead of ad-hoc `for node in …; do ssh`.
- Claiming a Ray equivalent still requires the gaps listed in `:adr/known-gaps`
  — above all a distributed object store and a resident worker pool. Until
  those exist, describe this as fleet batch execution, not as Ray.

## Follow-ups

1. Port the remaining murakumo control-plane commands to nbb entrypoints.
2. Audit the other SSH paths for the swallowed-exit-status defect.
3. Persist task runs to the kotoba Datom log (as `reconcile` already does).
4. Resident worker on `kotoba-server` to remove the per-task SSH setup cost."}]
